## Key terms (asset management — L5)

| Term | Description |
|------|-------------|
| Asset inventory | A list of hardware and software with type, serial number, location, user, purchase date, and warranty. The inventory is kept current for capacity planning, budgeting, and audit. Incomplete or outdated inventory leads to wrong decisions and audit findings. |
| Operational status | Categories such as in use, spare, under repair, retired, or disposed. Status is defined and tracked for capacity and replacement planning. Clear status definitions and regular updates support accurate reporting. |
| Costs and space | Total cost of ownership (TCO) including purchase, support, power, and space. Estimates are used for budget and capacity planning. Assumptions and calculation method should be documented for review and audit. |
| Asset management system | A tool or spreadsheet for inventory, status, tagging, and reporting. The system should align with organisational policy and be maintained so that data remains accurate and accessible for reporting and audit. |
| Tagging and labelling | Barcode or RFID tag on the asset and a corresponding record in the system. Tags are verified periodically and reconciled with the inventory. Consistent tagging reduces loss and supports audit. |
| Asset disposal | Data wipe (secure erase or overwrite), decommissioning from the network and user access, and recycle or dispose per organisational and regulatory policy. Disposal must be documented for audit and compliance. |
| Asset management report | Summary of inventory, operational status, costs, and disposal for management and audit. Report format and frequency should meet organisational and regulatory requirements. |

